From: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: sprd: give the VBC path a buffer a desktop can feed

sprd_pcm_hardware_v1 advertises buffer_bytes_max = AUDIO_BUFFER_BYTES_MAX,
and sprd_pcm_preallocate_dma_ddr32_buffer() allocates exactly that much, so
the two track each other by construction. At 64K for the VBC half that is
128K in total, i.e. 32768 frames, or 682 ms of 48 kHz S16_LE stereo.

That is enough for the short periods Android's HAL uses, but this hardware
wants long ones - anything under ~250 ms puts an audible artefact on every
period boundary - and 682 ms of buffer only holds two 250 ms periods. Two
periods means the writer must land a full period's worth of samples inside
one period every single time, with nothing in reserve, which is not a
promise a general purpose audio server on a loaded desktop can keep: MP3
and MP4 playback through PipeWire stutter on this board while the same
stream written straight to hw:0,0 does not.

The buffer comes out of the ddr32 audio-mem pool, which the DT sizes at
3 MiB (sprd,ddr32-dma = <0x87400000 0x300000>), so 128K was never the
constraint - it just was not revisited. Raise the VBC half to 192K, which
holds four 250 ms periods and is still under a tenth of the pool.

Nothing else scales with it: periods_max stays PAGE_SIZE / node size, so
the linked-list config allocation is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.h b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.h
index 973dfdfc3f5d..a682e203c2e7 100755
--- a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.h
+++ b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.h
@@ -23,7 +23,18 @@
 
 #define VBC_FIFO_FRAME_NUM		(80)
 
-#define VBC_BUFFER_BYTES_MAX		(64 * 1024)
+/*
+ * The DMA buffer is preallocated from the ddr32 audio-mem pool, which the
+ * DT sizes at 3 MiB (sprd,ddr32-dma), and both the preallocation and the
+ * advertised buffer_bytes_max come from AUDIO_BUFFER_BYTES_MAX, so the two
+ * cannot drift apart. 64K per direction left a 48 kHz S16 stereo stream
+ * with only 682 ms of buffer in total: ask for the 250 ms periods this
+ * hardware needs and ALSA can fit just two of them, i.e. the writer has to
+ * deliver a whole period inside one period with no slack, which a desktop
+ * audio server cannot promise. 192K is still a rounding error against the
+ * pool and leaves room for four such periods.
+ */
+#define VBC_BUFFER_BYTES_MAX		(192 * 1024)
 
 #define I2S_BUFFER_BYTES_MAX	(64 * 1024)
 
